WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:11.540
Ryanair is one of the most innovative companies
on the planet… and yet it also manages to

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.000
be one of the most hated.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.380
Yes, yes, we all have heard about TESLA or
the HYPERLOOP.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:19.611
But… let’s be honest, its going to be
years before we know if those companies are

00:00:19.611 --> 00:00:22.040
actually going to change lives...

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.230
Ryanair though, have already created a revolution.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:29.060
And I’m not just talking about Europe here,
Ryanair’s sway can be felt worldwide.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:33.430
But on the other side of things, it is hard
to find someone who doesn’t hate this airline.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:36.780
This is, partly, due to the efforts of its
CEO, Michael O’Leary.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.820
He seems to have really worked hard to becoming

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.000
the man you love to hate with quotes like
this one.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.970
“People make queues to go to the toilet…

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.070
how do we stop them?

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.280
Charge them for it!”

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.120
Or… this other one:

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.620
“Nobody wants to sit beside a really fattass
on board.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.890
We have been frankly astonished at the number
of

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:59.530
customers who don’t only want to tax fat
people but torture them.”

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:00.530
–Michael O´Leary

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:02.010
Well… there is actually another reason people
hate Ryanair...

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:05.079
If you have been following me here or in my
other channels, you might already know that

00:01:05.079 --> 00:01:06.110
I live in Prague,

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:07.110
Czech Republic.

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:10.420
My partners at VisualPolitik they live over
in Madrid, in Spain.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:16.330
Until this very month, Prague to Madrid flights
cost around $250.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:19.050
But this year, Ryanair came to town.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:23.159
And they offered Prague to Madrid routes for
as cheap as $90.

00:01:23.159 --> 00:01:25.250
And what did the other airlines do in response
to this?

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:28.390
Well, they were forced to lower their prices
too...

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:32.970
And the same happened all across Europe.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:37.690
Anywhere Ryanair operates, the rest of its
competitors have to put down their fare to

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:39.240
remain competitive.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:46.830
This means less profit margin or, even potential
bankruptcy.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.360
Just have a look at British Airways.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.460
In the year 2000, they carried over 46 million
passengers.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.380
8 years later, they don’t even carry the
33 million.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:02:00.060
And, of course, those big airlines like British
Airways, or Lufthansa, or Iberia spent a fortune

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.500
buying ads in the media.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.229
And because of this, they won the love of
journalists.

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:07.170
And you know who didn’t have much love from
journalists?

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.819
Well, that’d be Ryan Air, because they don’t
really buy any ads…

00:02:10.819 --> 00:02:14.630
Anyway, despite this bad reputation, there
are only a few companies that can claim to

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:18.209
have changed an entire industry the way Ryanair
did...

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:21.879
And, as I said before, this is not only a
European thing!

00:02:21.879 --> 00:02:24.499
The Ryanair effect can be felt all around
the world...

00:02:24.499 --> 00:02:31.739
Except perhaps in Latin America.

00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:34.060
So the first question we have to ask here
is…

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.280
How did Ryanair change the world?

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.840
Why do regions like Latin

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.629
America need a company like this?

00:02:39.629 --> 00:02:41.129
And most important of all…

00:02:41.129 --> 00:02:44.510
How does Ryanair manage to offer these ludicrously
low prices?

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:46.819
Well, today, we are going to answer all those
questions.

00:02:46.819 --> 00:02:51.450
But first, let’s take a look back at history!

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.400
WHY IS RYANAIR SO CHEAP?

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.700
Back in the 1980s, a Madrid to London flight
cost the equivalent of $700.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:01.279
Back then, in Spain, this was the average
monthly wage.

00:03:01.279 --> 00:03:03.680
Everyone understood that flying was a luxury...

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:10.900
At the end of the day… planes are expensive!

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.239
There was nothing people could do…

00:03:13.239 --> 00:03:16.379
Nevertheless, the reason why flights were
that expensive had little to

00:03:16.379 --> 00:03:20.379
do with technology and a lot to do with politics!

00:03:20.379 --> 00:03:23.579
Yeah, it’s true…

00:03:23.579 --> 00:03:28.510
Back then, air routes were based on agreements
between governments.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:32.920
For instance, the French Government and the
American one had a deal so only French and

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:36.239
American airlines could fly between Paris
and New York

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:37.239
City.

00:03:37.239 --> 00:03:40.510
The same happened with the rest of the European
countries.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:49.719
Airlines had, essentially, a monopoly over
the routes they operated.

00:03:49.719 --> 00:03:55.749
So now, why would they lower their fares if
they could make a fortune selling $700 tickets!?

00:03:55.749 --> 00:03:59.930
Back then were the times when only British
and Irish people could enjoy Ryanair's lower

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:00.930
fares.

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:05.579
But… in April of 1997, things changed forever.

00:04:05.579 --> 00:04:13.180
“From this month, any technically qualified
airline in the European Union (plus Norway

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:17.190
and Iceland) will be able to run services
inside any other country.”

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:20.750
All of a sudden, all those European Carriers
could compete against each other.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:21.750
But…

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:22.820
they didn’t know how.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:27.750
This is why they invested heavily on more
advertising and more luxuries: better food,

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:33.340
more comfortable seats, and even corporate
gifts to the passengers.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:36.920
Ryanair, instead, bet on a totally different
strategy.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:43.640
"The other airlines are asking how they can
put up fares.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:46.260
We are asking how we could get rid of them."

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:47.260
–Michael O´Leary

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:48.890
Saying this in 1997 was crazy.

00:04:48.890 --> 00:04:53.700
Of course, there were already other budget
airlines like the American airline South West.

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.270
But Ryanair wanted to go even further...

00:04:56.270 --> 00:05:00.360
This is why they found new ways to reduce
cost to the absolute minimum.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:05.140
For example, the landing cost depended, partly,
on the fares that airlines pay to the airports.

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:07.510
Landing at Brussels Central Airport is costly.

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:12.650
But 60km south, there is the Charleroi Airport,
which offers way lower landing fees.

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:18.730
Simply by choosing to fly to tht airport,
Ryanair saved a fortune.

00:05:18.730 --> 00:05:26.560
Another cost saving policy was utilizing the
same airplane model for all the routes, the

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:28.350
BOEING 737.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:32.590
Other companies like LUFTHANSA work with over
14 different types of airplanes.

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:38.080
This means they need 14 maintenance technicians,
each of them, specialized on each of the different

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:39.080
models.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:42.000
Ryanair, instead, can do the same with just
a few experts.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.891
This means that, while Lufthansa needs hours
between flights to do

00:05:44.891 --> 00:05:48.650
maintenance checks, Ryanair needs half that
time.

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:55.760
And, of course, add to this the rest of the
extra costs they save during the flights.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:57.200
Do you want to eat on the airplane?

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:58.200
Well that’s extra!

00:05:58.200 --> 00:05:59.300
Do you want to carry more baggage?

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:00.810
Yep, that’s extra too!

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:03.580
Want to choose your seat?

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:06.110
Every year, Ryanair finds new ways to reduce
costs.

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:10.300
For instance, a few years ago, on the back
of their seats they started posting adverts.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:14.830
But the real turning point to this company
came in the year 2000.

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:20.260
This was the time when Michael O’Leary made
a decision that changed the entire industry

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:21.660
for ever.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.830
"Screw the travel agents.

00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:25.140
Take the fuckers out and shoot them.

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.510
What have they done for passengers over the
years?"–Michael O´Leary

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:32.020
In the year 2000, Ryanair was the first company
to create a website where you could book tickets

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:33.020
online.

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:37.260
Prior to that, it was impossible to buy your
own plane tickets unless you lived close to

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:38.260
an airport.

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:40.920
So the travel agents where an essential middle
man.

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:45.830
And, of course, they charged commissions up
to a 30%...

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:50.400
Now, instead, passengers could just go to
the website, find the cheapest flight and

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:51.400
buy it.

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:52.840
Today, this is completely normal.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.950
But, back then, it was a revolution!

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:57.230
And it put lots of travel agents out of business.

00:06:57.230 --> 00:07:02.830
With all this, by the year 2010, Ryanair could
offer tickets for an average price of 40 USD.

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:03.830
That’s cheap.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:09.590
But it’s ridiculous when you consider the
$700 we were talking about before!

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:13.450
OK, ok, now you might be wondering…

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:16.650
Why are we saying cheap flights are such a
revolution?

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:17.650
Think about

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:22.090
it, since Ryanair expanded its business in
Europe, the amount of passengers has tripled.

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:30.020
Pay attention to the chart because we are
talking about much more than tourism here...

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:34.760
Thanks to the cheap flights, student exchange
programs have multiplied by 5 since 2007.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:38.960
In fact, if you are watching this video from
anywhere in Europe, you might have studied

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:42.110
abroad with an Erasmus program or have a friend
who did.

00:07:42.110 --> 00:07:46.020
The same goes with labour mobility, which
has tripled in just a decade.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:49.480
It would be hard to measure all the small
businesses that have been born thanks to the

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:50.480
cheap flights…

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:58.070
They can even allow a modest freelancer to
go find clients to another country.

00:07:58.070 --> 00:08:01.950
But wait a minute because there is another
detail we must keep in mind here!

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:06.120
If you look carefully, you will see that,
while flying has increased in Europe, Latin

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:08.540
America seems to be immune to those changes.

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:11.810
But didn’t I say that the Ryanair effect
was worldwide?

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:15.590
I mean, well, what happened to Latin America?

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:18.400
THE RYANAIR EFFECT

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:19.890
I know what you are thinking!

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:23.550
Ryanair just operates in Europe - how could
they have had such a big influence on the

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:24.550
rest of the world?

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:25.550
Well…

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:27.560
I’ll let you draw your own conclusions…

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:29.810
AirASIA was an airline owned by the Malaysian
Government.

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:32.139
At the beginning of the 2000s, they were on
the

00:08:32.139 --> 00:08:33.380
verge of bankruptcy.

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:39.180
Then in 2001, the Malaysian businessman, TONY
FERNANDEZ bought it and followed the ‘Ryanair

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:41.469
model’ that had started to work in Europe.

00:08:41.469 --> 00:08:46.040
AirAsia adopted the slogan: ‘Now everyone
can fly’ and he offered the first low cost

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:47.129
flights on the continent.

00:08:47.129 --> 00:08:52.670
Suddenly, Kuala Lumpur to Macao could be done
for less than $90.

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:58.950
As happened in Europe, Asia then saw the birth
of many other low cost airlines - from LION

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:02.449
AIR, in Indonessia, to CEBU PACIFIC in the
Philippines.

00:09:02.449 --> 00:09:08.360
Even a country as poor and as big as INDIA
has seen the amount of passengers triple in

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:12.019
just 10 years.

00:09:12.019 --> 00:09:15.600
But hold on a minute because there is this
part of the planet which seems to be immune

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:19.700
from this Ryanair effect... and that area
as we mentioned is Latin America…

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:26.680
For example, a cheap flight between Bogota,
in Colombia, and Mexico City, costs over $460.

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:30.829
Just compare that with the $86 we pay for
going from Kuala Lumpur to Taipei.

00:09:30.829 --> 00:09:37.470
And, in case you want to check the distance,
both are, more or less, the same.

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:41.860
And the same goes if you want to go from Lima,
in Peru, to La Paz in Bolivia.

00:09:41.860 --> 00:09:44.970
This is a 3 hours flight that costs over $600.

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:50.060
And yes, this is more than the average monthly
wage in most Latin American countries.

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:51.470
So now you might wonder…

00:09:51.470 --> 00:09:55.370
Why is it so damn expensive to fly in Latin
America?

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:57.430
Why don’t they have some Ryanair-like company?

00:09:57.430 --> 00:09:59.829
The answer lies, again, in politics.

00:09:59.829 --> 00:10:03.620
In other words, legislation in the region
has made it impossible for a Ryanair-like

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:10.060
company to appear.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:14.639
If an airline wants to work in Brazil, they
need to have, at least, 20% of their capital,

00:10:14.639 --> 00:10:16.180
owned by Brazilians.

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:18.620
And 2 years ago, this percentage was even
higher...

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:23.300
This is why, when AVIANCA, a Colombian carrier,
wanted to open to markets in the country,

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:27.540
the government forced them to start a brand
new company with their headquarters in Brazil.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:29.350
Thus, AVIANCA and AVIANCA

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:34.040
BRAZIL are two entirely different companies
despite the fact that they belong the same

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:35.040
group...

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:40.970
But Brazil is not the only country that protects
their own airlines...

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:48.529
Until the year 2016, Argentinians who wanted
to fly could only choose between two companies:

00:10:48.529 --> 00:10:54.230
ARGENTINA LATAM, a private carrier, and AEROLINEAS
ARGENTINAS, a state owned airline.

00:10:54.230 --> 00:11:00.339
For decades, the CRISTINA FERNANDEZ DE KIRCHNER
administration made sure no other airline

00:11:00.339 --> 00:11:02.019
could compete with these airlines...

00:11:02.019 --> 00:11:03.759
And the same goes for the airports...

00:11:03.759 --> 00:11:07.449
Remember when I said that Ryanair could cut
cost thanks to taking off and landing at cheap

00:11:07.449 --> 00:11:08.449
airports?

00:11:08.449 --> 00:11:13.050
Well… this is possible because in Europe,
1 out of 4 airports are managed by a marriage

00:11:13.050 --> 00:11:19.579
between the governments and private companies.

00:11:19.579 --> 00:11:20.649
Thanks to this, airports must

00:11:20.649 --> 00:11:24.019
compete with each others by offering better
services and lower fares.

00:11:24.019 --> 00:11:28.209
It’s not by chance that half of European
air traffic comes from private airports.

00:11:28.209 --> 00:11:30.480
Latin America is a different animal though.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:33.399
Most of their airports are managed, solely,
by the government.

00:11:33.399 --> 00:11:37.079
If there is no competition, they simply have
no interest in reducing fares...

00:11:37.079 --> 00:11:38.420
In other words…

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:41.259
Ryanair has failed to have an effect on this
region because of politics.

00:11:41.259 --> 00:11:43.129
And what are the consequences?

00:11:43.129 --> 00:11:47.190
Well… despite the fact that many Latin American
countries have grown economically in the

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:55.639
past years, flying is still a privilege for
the rich.

00:11:55.639 --> 00:11:57.290
So now the question is…

00:11:57.290 --> 00:12:00.449
Do you think Latin America should liberalize
its air space?

00:12:00.449 --> 00:12:04.769
Do you think everyone could benefit from having
a company like Ryanir operate in his or her

00:12:04.769 --> 00:12:05.769
country?

00:12:05.769 --> 00:12:08.740
Well, please, leave your answers in the comment
section below.

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:12.600
